WEBVTT

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:20.220
Leave it to Beaver, starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont, Tony Dow, and Jerry Mathers as the Beaver.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.060
Couldn't you at least let us try, Mr. Merkle? We'll work real hard.

00:00:26.030 --> 00:00:26.830
We sure will.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.260
Now, you're only on trial.

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:35.160
You make one mistake and I'll skin you alive, both of them.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:36.700
Yes, sir.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:38.450
Yes, sir.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:38.530
Yes, sir.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.620
I was straightening up the closet and you shut me in.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.900
Sorry, I didn't see you.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.900
I have a sneaking suspicion you did.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.060
but I can't prove it.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:35.230
Boys home yet?

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.090
Uh-huh. They're waiting for you.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:39.060
They want to talk to you about something.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.160
They want to talk to me about it.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.190
He didn't say, but it seems whatever it is,

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.470
it's for your ears alone.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:45.980
Well, I can understand the boys wanting

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.020
to confide in their father.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:49.840
They know their old dad can solve any problem.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.880
They know that given the opportunity...

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:52.940
I think they want something.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.260
Should have left you in the closet.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.000
Be ready in half an hour.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:02.870
Can you see it again, Wally?

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:03.780
Take it easy.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.110
Here he comes.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:12.360
Oh, hi, fellas.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:13.360
Hi, Dad.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:14.430
Hi, Dad.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.690
Have a nice day at the office?

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.640
Yeah, a pretty nice day.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:19.840
That's good, isn't it, Wally?

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:20.680
Yeah.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:27.210
Boys, your mother said you wanted to see me about something.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.080
Yeah.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:29.900
Well, you know, Dad,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.160
We've been late for school the last couple of mornings now.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:34.520
Late for school, huh?

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:37.080
Well, we can't have that, can we?

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:40.850
Yeah, and we've been late ever since the tire fell off our old bike.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.880
Oh, well, I think we could arrange to put a new tire on your old bike.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Would it be better to put a new bike on the old tire?

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:52.070
A new bike?

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:53.220
Yeah, Dad.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:02:59.400
Well, that's a...

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.360
It's currently a very nice looking bicycle.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.520
Should be for $52.98.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.420
We've been saving for it for a long time, Dad.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.670
You mean you boys have saved enough money to buy this bike?

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:17.600
Well, not exactly.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.840
We thought maybe you could chip in a little.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.110
Well, I think I could help out a little.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.360
How much have you saved?

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:27.320
I think around $6, almost.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.920
We went to the movies a couple of times.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.260
I kind of ate into it.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.400
Well, now, wait a minute, fellows.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.540
I can't just give you almost $50.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:40.420
Oh, we'll pay you back, Dad.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.680
I'll cut the grass for the next five years.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:46.530
And I'll pick up my room for the rest of my life.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.650
Well, that's a very tempting offer, boys.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.460
But after all, I can't just hand you over $50.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.540
You know, your old dad's not made of money.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.150
Gee, Dad, how else are we going to get to buy you

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.310
if you don't buy it for us?

00:03:58.960 --> 00:03:59.960
Well, I was, um...

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:03.660
Let me tell you a little story.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:08.860
Everything all right upstairs?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:09.920
Oh, sure.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:13.520
I was, uh, I was just telling the boys a little story

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:14.840
about my college days.

00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:17.580
About the time you fumbled the ball on the one yard line?

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.240
Oh, no, of course not.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.100
Why would I tell them that?

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.480
No, they, uh, they were asking me for a bicycle.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.700
So I told them about the time I wanted a car,

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.460
and when I got myself a job and earned it.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:29.320
What?

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:29.980
I wish you wouldn't.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.260
and tell the boys things like that.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.280
They take everything you say so seriously.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:39.640
Well, I don't think you have to worry about it.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.300
I don't think they want a new bicycle badly

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.440
enough to go to work for it.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:52.650
I ought to give you guys no paper route.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.680
Your clothes are too good.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:56.740
You look like the kind of kids who'd

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:57.940
quit after the first week.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:59.980
Gee, Mr. Merkle, we never

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.860
quit any other job we never even had no another job who sent you guys down here

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:13.780
Jack Pistel he used to work for you pistol and he the kid who moved away

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:20.650
without paying me no sir he fell off his bike and broke his leg oh yeah I knew

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:24.290
he did something to mess me up couldn't you at least let us try mr.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.160
We'll work real hard.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:27.860
We sure will.

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:29.350
We won't break our legs.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.500
And leave it with me, Wally.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:33.210
Yeah.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.710
We'll dump the papers off at your house every day.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.010
You fold them and wrap them and have them delivered by five.

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:42.270
On Saturday, you turn in your refunds

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.230
and we make our collection.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:45.850
Now, you're only on trial.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:47.230
And remember something.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:50.350
You make one mistake and I'll skin you alive.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:51.440
Both of you.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:52.720
Yes, sir.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.170
Now, you sign these papers here

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:57.430
and you can start tomorrow night.

00:05:58.440 --> 00:05:58.740
Here.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:06.500
Uh-huh. It's the shelf paper you asked for three days ago.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:08.560
Oh, darling, you shouldn't have done it.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.630
And I do mean you shouldn't have done it.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.810
I bought some at the market this morning.

00:06:12.930 --> 00:06:15.790
Oh. Well, of course, we can't have too much shelf paper.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:16.770
What's up?

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.720
I planted some marigolds.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.430
Man of the nursery said it was the wrong time of year.

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.620
We did that on purpose.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:34.160
It's too late now, dear.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.020
What do you suppose these are?

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:40.410
They're newspapers.

00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:42.290
Well, those are for us, Dad.

00:06:42.670 --> 00:06:43.820
Yeah, those are for us.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:44.800
Yeah, thanks a lot.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.560
Hey, boys, wait a minute.

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:53.720
What's this all about?

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:55.330
It's our paper, Dad.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:57.130
We're going to run back and surprise you

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.270
just like you did your father.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.840
But boys, you get home from school pretty late as it is.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.160
Isn't that a lot of papers to deliver?

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:07.500
Only 58.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:09.300
But Beaver.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:11.710
Gee, Mom, there's nothing to deliver in papers.

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:13.710
You just fold them up and throw them at people.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.870
Wally, let's go and wrap them.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:19.760
But Ward.

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:21.550
Well, I think it's a good idea.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:23.110
It gives them a sense of responsibility.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.650
Ward, every time the boys start something like this,

00:07:25.670 --> 00:07:27.210
you end up doing the job for them.

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:29.110
Like the time they were going to sell that perfume

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:29.970
to get a movie.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.140
Well, it was just that one time.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.450
How about the time they were gonna get $2 for cutting the Donaldson's lawn?

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.580
You rented a power mower, went over and did the job for them.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:40.300
Julie, we were a lot younger then.

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.560
Now, believe me, this time I'm not gonna so much as lift a hand to help them.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:45.220
Ha!

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:58.940
But look, that's the saddest thing I've ever seen.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.960
Well, I just think it's going to be too much for them.

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:03.480
Well, they'll be all right.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.680
That's the way Bernard Baruch got started.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:21.430
Hey, that was pretty good.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:22.870
Lucky the window was open.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.430
Shouldn't we get that one, Wally?

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.340
Nah, the bushes are the first place people love.

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:44.150
What are you doing out here all by yourself?

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:45.960
All the newspapers, Mom.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:46.900
Where's Wally?

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:49.830
He had to stay after school for a makeup test.

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:54.640
Well, Beaver, you can't deliver all of these newspapers by yourself.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:56.870
I've got them, Mom.

00:08:57.900 --> 00:08:59.900
If I don't get these papers out by five...

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.970
from the newspaper is going to skin us alive, both of us.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:06.720
Beaver, it's getting late.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.160
Well, you'll never get all of these newspapers

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:11.300
folded by yourself in time to deliver them.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.260
Then I guess I got to get skinned alive.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:16.540
I don't think I'm going to like that.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:22.140
Look, Beaver, why don't you try folding one like this?

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:22.640
See?

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:27.530
It's real neat, Mom.

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:41.940
Paper's real swell.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.150
Were you a paper boy?

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:45.280
No, I was never a paper boy.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.410
But at school, I used to fold napkins for the sorority house.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:50.380
Here.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.740
What is it, Mom?

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:56.830
Goodness, it's almost five.

00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:59.770
Oh, if we don't get these papers out by five,

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:16.000
Well, Beaver, your father isn't home yet, but come on, I'll help you.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:18.290
Gee, thanks, Mom.

00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:22.740
But, Beaver, your father wanted Wally and you to do this job, so, uh...

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:25.680
Well, let's not mention it, huh?

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:26.540
Okay.

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:29.970
Boy, well, I'd be glad when Wally has roots.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:51.300
tomorrow night why didn't she come in the back way cuz I didn't want to get the

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:58.260
kitchen wet dad water doesn't show so much on rugs of course not well old man

00:10:58.260 --> 00:10:59.780
Merkel called from the newspaper

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.340
He said you forgot to deliver nine or 10 papers tonight.

00:11:02.920 --> 00:11:05.320
Yeah, my list got all wet, and some of the customers

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:06.140
ran together.

00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:08.340
I got another list upstairs.

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:10.990
Well, he seemed pretty upset about it.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.580
Yeah, that old man, Merkle, he's a mean guy.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.330
He's always firing kids.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.440
I better get the other list and go out again.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:18.260
Now, wait a minute.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:21.000
You mean you're going out in this rain?

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:23.910
Gee, Dad, I've got to do my job, like you said.

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:25.490
Yeah, yeah.

00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:26.730
Well, look, I'll tell you what.

00:11:27.050 --> 00:11:29.550
You go and get the list, and I'll get the car out of the garage.

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:29.850
Gee.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:30.600
Thanks, Dad.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.880
Did you get all your papers wet?

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.780
Well, some did, but that's OK.

00:11:34.900 --> 00:11:36.500
They always give us a lot of extra ones.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:42.060
Oh, Wally, about my helping you this one time,

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:44.660
no need for your mother to know about it.

00:11:45.030 --> 00:11:46.610
OK, I'll tell her you didn't help me.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:47.330
No, no, no, no.

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:49.390
Don't be dishonest.

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:50.870
Just don't mention it, that's all.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.910
Julian, what's this check for $620 from the city cab?

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.840
Well, uh, you don't take cabs.

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:04.630
No?

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:07.100
No, this one's yours, dear.

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.300
It's, uh, dated last Tuesday.

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:12.540
Uh, you been shopping over on the expensive side of town again?

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:17.390
Well, Ward, you were so insistent that the boys handle this paper route by themselves

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:21.310
that I wasn't going to tell you, but, well, the beaver got stuck on Tuesday night,

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:25.430
and, well, I'm afraid we delivered the papers by cab.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.970
June, you delivered 58 five-cent papers in a six-...

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.900
There's nothing else I could do.

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:37.220
June, we've got to let the boys learn to stand on their own two feet.

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:41.210
Like the rainy night you took the car out and helped Wally deliver the papers?

00:12:41.630 --> 00:12:46.410
Uh, yeah, that's about as good an example as I can think of.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:50.800
Guess we're both guilty. Sometimes there's nothing else you can do.

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:54.320
Now, June, I still insist we shouldn't help the boys.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:55.080
I agree.

00:12:55.700 --> 00:12:59.920
And, uh, when we do help them, uh, we should help them without their knowing we're helping.

00:13:00.890 --> 00:13:01.330
I agree.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:08.160
18, 19.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:10.840
Wally, you've got an awful lot of papers

00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:12.000
left over from last week.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:13.720
Sure, Beaver, these are extras.

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:17.020
They're old papers, and we already paid for them.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.530
Well, they call them refunds.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.720
We take them back to old man Merkel tomorrow

00:13:21.720 --> 00:13:22.740
and get our money back.

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:26.090
You know, Wally, I like that old man Merkel.

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:28.440
You want to get money back from him.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:33.120
Have we made any mistakes yet?

00:13:34.810 --> 00:13:35.910
Well, I don't think so.

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.420
Would old man Markle be mad about that

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:40.440
valve grip in your pants?

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:41.920
Well, I don't know.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:43.690
We better not tell him about it.

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:47.660
It isn't as much fun as I thought it'd be.

00:13:48.930 --> 00:13:50.720
I wonder why old people do it so much.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.000
They have to.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.500
If they had fun like kids, people would say they were silly.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:04.100
Sure, who'd ever touch a bunch of last week's papers?

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:11.460
Wally and the Beaver haven't been there, Mrs. Anderson?

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:15.380
Well, look, if Chester does see them,

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:16.760
would you have them call us, please?

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:19.470
All right, thank you. Goodbye.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.940
June, you know what I found out in the garage?

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:25.300
Wally and the Beaver's newspapers all folded, wrapped, and undelivered.

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:26.600
I know, dear. I saw them.

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.440
I've been calling all over town trying to find them.

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:29.980
It's...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.540
Saturday, they've been out playing,

00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:33.940
and they've probably forgotten all about the newspapers.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.580
Well, they picked a great day to forget,

00:14:35.740 --> 00:14:38.420
Saturday afternoon with half the town waiting for its newspaper.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:40.180
Oh, Ward, I hope they get here in time.

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.760
They've had that job for a whole week,

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:43.120
and I'd hate to see them lose it.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:43.860
Want to know something else?

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:45.680
I don't think old man Merkel is the type

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:46.960
to listen to excuses, either.

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.540
Oh, Ward, you know they're not going to get here on time.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.130
What are we going to do about it?

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:56.900
Well, I guess we're going to get the car out

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:58.920
and deliver those papers as fast as we can.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:12.820
Yeah, we got word about the newspapers.

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.800
That's why we came down to the newspaper office.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:15.760
Well, you kids are new.

00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:17.560
The Saturday edition is always late.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:18.300
That's on account of it.

00:15:18.340 --> 00:15:19.640
It's got all the ball scores in it.

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:21.380
We'll remember that next time.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:23.170
He went up out of refund copies.

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:24.170
We got them in the garage.

00:15:24.310 --> 00:15:25.170
You want to take them with you?

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:27.050
No, you take them down to Merkel on Monday,

00:15:27.110 --> 00:15:28.150
and he'll give you your money back.

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.670
And you guys better get in the ball.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.160
And Merkel's a monster.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:05.920
Oh, no. It says it's going to rain again tomorrow.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:11.740
Again? Dear, this is last Tuesday's paper they just delivered.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:14.640
Wait till I call that crazy newspaper office.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:56.280
Mark's paper.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:16:59.860
Yeah.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.000
Yeah, well, yeah, well, gee, Mr. Merkle,

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:08.100
we haven't delivered the papers yet.

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:10.740
Complaints?

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:13.810
I don't know what the people are talking about.

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:17.560
No, Mr. Merkle, we're not being wise, guys.

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:23.560
Oh, well, all right, OK.

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:27.040
What's the matter, Wally?

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:29.880
Well, Mr. Merkle just fired us.

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:32.360
What'd we do?

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:34.970
He's yelling so loud, I'm not sure.

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:40.720
I think he said we delivered last week's papers today.

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.470
How could we do that?

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:43.450
They're out in the garage.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:44.010
Yeah.

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:46.810
I don't know what happened.

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:53.910
Well, I didn't think we'd make it.

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.970
Neither did I, especially when we ran short

00:17:56.970 --> 00:17:59.250
and had to buy extra copies at the news den.

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:07.140
Well, there you are.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:08.200
Oh, hi, Mom.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:08.860
Hi, Dad.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:09.680
Hi.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:15.680
Boys, I must say, I'm a little disappointed in both you

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:16.660
and your paper route.

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:19.170
That was Mr. Merkel.

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:20.270
He just fired us.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:21.460
He fired you?

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:22.150
What for?

00:18:22.660 --> 00:18:24.380
Well, gee, Dad, it wasn't our fault.

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.420
Somebody delivered last week's papers.

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:28.550
Yeah, to all our customers.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:34.860
Last week's papers.

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:35.660
Uh-huh.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:37.980
We left them all out in the garage, on the workbench.

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:39.950
It's all wrapped up in the raincoats.

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:40.690
Oh.

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:42.660
Oh.

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:43.920
Uh-oh.

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:45.960
Circle seats are out on the workbench.

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:49.550
Boys, they're not on the workbench.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:51.040
Well, then where are they, Mom?

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:57.480
Well, we, I mean, we, this is one of those things

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:58.980
that a father can explain best.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:20.340
um will you sit down boys no thanks no thanks i think i will tell you about the time i fumbled

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.020
the ball on the one yard line well

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:34.170
Boys, we'll have breakfast as soon as your father gets back.

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.160
Did Dad go someplace this early?

00:19:36.670 --> 00:19:38.410
Well, he had to go on a little errand.

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:39.600
OK, Mom.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:46.260
Boys, you know you have a very wonderful father, don't you?

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:47.470
Oh, sure, Mom.

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:48.740
Oh, sure, Mom.

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:52.440
And you know, the mistake yesterday was just as much

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:53.600
my fault as it was his.

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:55.320
Oh, that's OK, Mom.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:56.880
It was just one of those things.

00:19:59.140 --> 00:19:59.980
Yeah, I was just one of those things.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:00.700
One of those things.

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:10.760
What?

00:20:16.850 --> 00:20:17.390
What, babe?

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:20.600
You would have thought one of them

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:21.920
would look at the dates on those papers.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:23.920
We were just trying to help.

00:20:24.250 --> 00:20:25.990
Some parents wouldn't have even done that.

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.760
Yeah, I guess we can't get too mad at them.

00:20:29.130 --> 00:20:29.510
Uh-uh.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:36.710
always get a new job. Yeah, I guess that's a lot better than getting new parents.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:58.700
I see you, Jack. Hey, look at you in a minute.

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:05.510
Uh, son, uh, I'd like to speak to, uh, old man Merkle.

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:07.060
I'm old man Merkle.

00:21:07.610 --> 00:21:08.650
Keep your shirt on.

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:14.240
What's your problem, Jack?

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:19.300
Well, uh, I'd like to talk to you about the two Cleaver boys.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:20.160
Cleaver?

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:22.650
Oh, you mean those two kids who goofed up the route

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:23.290
last night, huh?

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:24.830
You get last week's paper, too?

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:26.390
Oh, no, no, no, I didn't.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:29.120
No, you see, uh, I'm their father.

00:21:30.280 --> 00:21:30.540
So?

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:35.560
No, so it wasn't their fault about the mix-up.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:37.000
What do you mean it wasn't their fault?

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:38.120
It was their route, wasn't it?

00:21:38.890 --> 00:21:39.290
Yes.

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:40.550
Yes, it was.

00:21:40.690 --> 00:21:44.940
But you see, I delivered those papers.

00:21:46.980 --> 00:21:48.780
Why would you do a thing like that, Jack?

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.770
My wife and I were trying to help the boys,

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:54.450
and we got hold of the wrong papers.

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:55.810
And don't call me Jack.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:57.270
OK, mister.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:21:58.940
What do you want me to do?

00:21:59.150 --> 00:21:59.970
I want you to give the boys a call.

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:03.410
Seems to me that even a man with your limited perspicacity

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:05.430
should be able to see that's the only fair thing to do.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:07.740
Are you trying to insult me, Jack?

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:19.080
Look, son, my company does a good deal of advertising with this paper.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:24.680
Now, uh, do you think we should settle this here, or should I go upstairs?

00:22:26.810 --> 00:22:29.730
Well, what guarantee have I got that the route won't be messed up again?

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:32.480
You've got my word for it, Jack.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:36.120
Well, OK.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:42.590
Well, I got the job back.

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:43.900
Good, how'd you do it?

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:46.030
I let a fellow call me Jack.

00:22:47.090 --> 00:22:47.980
How are we?

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:48.580
Beaver.

00:22:52.700 --> 00:22:53.780
Good news for you, boys.

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:54.840
Got your job back.

00:22:55.820 --> 00:22:56.200
Oh.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:22:58.020
What's the matter?

00:22:58.580 --> 00:22:59.980
Your father went to a lot of trips.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:01.570
Yeah, I know.

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:03.980
But, well, see, I just talked to Chester.

00:23:04.020 --> 00:23:06.500
And, well, he's going to take us down to the supermarket.

00:23:07.310 --> 00:23:08.550
Yeah, we're going to talk for a minute

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:10.130
about packing boxes on weekends.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:14.760
Well, but, boys, your paper routes.

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:16.540
Well, that's Chester's father now.

00:23:16.600 --> 00:23:17.420
We've got to get going.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:19.240
Yeah, we don't want to mess up another job.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:20.820
All right, boys, look.

00:23:24.750 --> 00:23:26.070
Well, what do we do now?

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:29.560
Well, I guess you fold and hair up.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:49.290
They're out unpacking it.

00:23:50.330 --> 00:23:51.090
Yeah, it's funny.

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.680
I, uh, I know I encouraged them to earn their bike,

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:54.600
and I'm proud they did.

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:58.170
But it does kind of put their father in a non-essential class.

00:23:58.710 --> 00:23:59.850
Oh, don't be silly.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.790
Go on, see if you can help them.

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:03.190
Well, maybe I will.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.290
I'll just sort of wander out that way.

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:21.020
That's a mighty fine looking bicycle, boys.

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:22.790
It's real neat, Dad.

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:25.960
You know, when my brother and I got our bicycle together,

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:27.540
we had a terrible argument about who

00:24:27.540 --> 00:24:28.640
was going to get the first ride.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:29.980
We're not arguing, Dad.

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:32.040
Mom, we just decided that.

00:24:32.750 --> 00:24:34.350
We want you to have the first ride.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:44.060
I guess you're kind of embarrassed riding a kid's bike, huh?

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:47.360
Me?

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:49.310
Not on your life.

00:25:00.830 --> 00:25:01.070
Thank you.

00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:30.820
You
